WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:00.387
Continuo.

2
00:00:00.624 --> 00:00:01.932
How it works, in three minutes.

3
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.598
What if your band was always there?

4
00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:08.346
Play guitar, and a band plays along: drums

5
00:00:08.346 --> 00:00:10.604
and bass, live, following your timing and

6
00:00:10.604 --> 00:00:10.849
feel.

7
00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.156
This is how it works, in three minutes.

8
00:00:16.593 --> 00:00:17.822
Here is the whole chain.

9
00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:20.382
A guitar, a pickup under its strings, a

10
00:00:20.382 --> 00:00:22.685
small board, a chip, a laptop, and the

11
00:00:22.685 --> 00:00:23.146
speakers.

12
00:00:23.581 --> 00:00:25.424
Pluck a note, and it travels left to

13
00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:27.739
right: string, pickup, board, chip,

14
00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:28.637
laptop, speakers.

15
00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.416
Four of us built it.

16
00:00:30.732 --> 00:00:32.786
Sena plays, and built the board that

17
00:00:32.786 --> 00:00:33.787
follows the pickup.

18
00:00:34.103 --> 00:00:36.105
Dini and Felix built what runs on the

19
00:00:36.105 --> 00:00:36.369
chip.

20
00:00:36.685 --> 00:00:38.529
Jaime built the band on the laptop.

21
00:00:38.845 --> 00:00:40.636
And you hear it from the speakers.

22
00:00:44.857 --> 00:00:46.145
A guitar has six strings.

23
00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:48.723
A microphone would hear them all at once,

24
00:00:48.723 --> 00:00:49.032
mixed.

25
00:00:49.342 --> 00:00:51.198
This pickup has one coil under each

26
00:00:51.198 --> 00:00:53.157
string, so each string gets its own

27
00:00:53.157 --> 00:00:53.518
signal.

28
00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:56.147
Play a chord, and it arrives as six single

29
00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:58.157
notes: six strings, six signals.

30
00:01:00.202 --> 00:01:02.155
The chip watches all six strings at once.

31
00:01:02.441 --> 00:01:04.537
For each one it finds when a note starts,

32
00:01:04.537 --> 00:01:06.586
how hard it was played, and when it ends.

33
00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:09.111
And which note it is: it slides a copy of

34
00:01:09.111 --> 00:01:11.064
the wave along itself until the two line

35
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:11.207
up.

36
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:13.542
Dini built the part that finds each note's

37
00:01:13.542 --> 00:01:15.638
start, strength and end, Felix the part

38
00:01:15.638 --> 00:01:16.686
that finds which note.

39
00:01:17.398 --> 00:01:19.374
Then each note becomes a few small

40
00:01:19.374 --> 00:01:21.859
messages, sent down a cable to the laptop.

41
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.584
On the laptop, your notes land on a grid

42
00:01:27.584 --> 00:01:29.387
of sixteenth notes, at your tempo.

43
00:01:29.688 --> 00:01:30.539
This line is now.

44
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:33.145
A drummer looks back over everything you

45
00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:35.375
have played so far, and decides the next

46
00:01:35.375 --> 00:01:37.761
sixteenth before it happens: which drums

47
00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:38.642
to hit, if any.

48
00:01:38.953 --> 00:01:41.287
It listens to the guitar, never to its own

49
00:01:41.287 --> 00:01:41.598
drums.

50
00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:42.797
Then a bassist.

51
00:01:43.076 --> 00:01:45.024
It hears the guitar, the notes you last

52
00:01:45.024 --> 00:01:46.972
played, and what the drummer just chose,

53
00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:49.322
and picks one of twelve notes, counted up

54
00:01:49.322 --> 00:01:51.386
from the lowest guitar note, so it works

55
00:01:51.386 --> 00:01:51.913
in any key.

56
00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:54.545
Both go to the speakers, placed exactly on

57
00:01:54.545 --> 00:01:54.985
the beat.

58
00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:57.238
Then the next sixteenth, and the next.

59
00:02:00.777 --> 00:02:01.268
Why ahead?

60
00:02:01.563 --> 00:02:03.580
A sixteenth note at a hundred and twenty

61
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.399
beats per minute lasts a hundred and

62
00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:06.629
twenty-five milliseconds.

63
00:02:06.924 --> 00:02:08.842
You pluck a string at the start of one.

64
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.618
By design, the note leaves the chip for

65
00:02:11.618 --> 00:02:13.890
the laptop about forty-five milliseconds

66
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.222
later.

67
00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:16.938
That is a design value, not yet measured

68
00:02:16.938 --> 00:02:17.547
end to end.

69
00:02:18.065 --> 00:02:20.117
By then a good part of the sixteenth is

70
00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:20.783
already gone.

71
00:02:21.091 --> 00:02:23.091
A band that waited for each note would

72
00:02:23.091 --> 00:02:23.860
always be late.

73
00:02:24.168 --> 00:02:26.527
So it does not wait: it decides the next

74
00:02:26.527 --> 00:02:28.373
sixteenth before it happens, from

75
00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:29.655
everything played so far.

76
00:02:29.963 --> 00:02:31.758
It is always one note ahead of you.

77
00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:43.595
On the seventeenth of October the guitar

78
00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:45.837
reached the band live for the first time.

79
00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:48.571
Four more short videos follow the signal,

80
00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:49.392
stage by stage.

81
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.235
First, the pickup: hearing each string.

